= Epistemic status — Technical =

== Dimensions ==
An epistemic state can be evaluated along non-equivalent dimensions:

<pre>
truth value
justification
knowledge
warrant
credence
reliability
safety
sensitivity
defeat status
coherence
evidential support
</pre>

A multidimensional representation is usually more faithful than forcing all of these into one scalar score.

== Credence ==
Formal epistemology may represent an agent's graded belief as:

<pre>
Cr(p) ∈ [0,1]
</pre>

Probabilistic coherence constrains relationships among credences but does not by itself establish whether p is known or justified.

Calibration compares probabilistic forecasts/credences with frequencies or outcomes over suitable reference sets, but calibration is not a general replacement for epistemic evaluation.

== Reliability ==
For a belief-forming process type <code>M</code>, reliabilist theories evaluate a truth-conducive performance relation in a relevant environment.

The generality/reference-class problem arises because one token belief can be classified under many process types with different reliabilities.

== Modal status ==
Sensitivity condition, schematically:

<pre>
if p were false → S would not believe p
</pre>

Safety condition, schematically:

<pre>
in nearby cases where S believes p in the same way → p is not false
</pre>

Neither condition alone has achieved universal acceptance as a complete analysis of knowledge.

== Defeat and higher-order evidence ==
Status can be altered by:
* rebutting defeaters;
* undercutting defeaters;
* evidence about evidence;
* evidence about the reliability of one's own reasoning.

This makes epistemic status temporally indexed and context-sensitive.

== Project taxonomies ==
Operational controlled vocabularies can be useful when they define:
* assignment criteria;
* evidence requirements;
* review triggers;
* lifecycle semantics;
* whether labels are ordered or orthogonal.

They should not be exported as philosophical natural kinds without argument.

